20:29:59 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: Even when your thermostat is set to the same 72°F year‑round, your body can feel noticeably colder in winter because of several factors that go beyond air temperature: 1. **Radiant Heat Loss** Your body constantly exchanges heat with surrounding surfaces (walls, windows, floors, furniture). In winter, even well‑insulated exterior walls and windows are colder than in summer. Because you radiate heat to those cooler surfaces, you *feel* cooler—your skin senses the net loss of radiant heat, not just the air temperature. In summer, those same surfaces are warmer, reducing that radiant heat drain. 2. **Lower Indoor Humidity** Cold outside air holds little moisture. When that air is brought indoors and heated to 72°F, its relative humidity drops dramatically—often below 30%. Dry air speeds up evaporation of moisture from your skin, producing a cooling effect (just like sweat evaporating). In summer, indoor air at 72°F usually has much higher humidity, so evaporative cooling is less noticeable. 3. **Drafts and Cold Spots** Windows, doors, and even electrical outlets often leak tiny amounts of cold air in winter. Even a slight draft across your skin accelerates heat loss through convection, making a stable 72°F reading feel chilly. Thermostats measure air near them, not the cold pockets where you’re sitting. 4. **Adaptation and Clothing** Your body acclimates to seasonal temperatures. In summer, you’re used to heat, and light clothing leaves skin more exposed but the environment feels “normal.” In winter, you may be wearing lighter indoor clothes after coming in from heavy outdoor layers, but your body’s thermal expectations have shifted. Even if you dress the same, the combination of factors above can override the thermostat number. So while the air might be 72°F, your body “feels” its thermal environment as a combination of air temperature, radiation, humidity, and air movement—all of which tend to work against comfort in winter.
