ion). The air temperature is still 72 °F, but the *rate of heat removal* increases. ### 3. Lower humidity reduces the insulating microclimate Winter indoor air is very dry. While you’re not sweating, your skin is always losing a small amount of moisture. In dry air, evaporation happens faster, pulling heat away more efficiently. This, too, increases your body’s cooling rate without changing the air temperature reading. **Bottom line:** The “feels cold” you experience at the same 72 °F is not a psychological illusion or a sensory glitch—it’s a genuine physical difference. Your body is losing heat faster to cold walls and windows via radiation, and often via tiny drafts and faster evaporation, so your thermal comfort plummets even though the air thermometer stays happy at 72. The proper engineering term for what your body actually “feels” is **operative temperature**, which combines air temperature and mean radiant temperature. In winter, the mean radiant temperature is lower, so the operative temperature can be 3–8 °F colder than the air temperature. That’s why you need the heat set higher in winter to achieve the same sensation.
